# Site Change Impact

The Site Change Impact report answers the question "what does my glucose do
around an infusion site change?". It lays all your site changes on top of each
other, so a pattern that repeats becomes clear.

## What it shows

### The header

Above the chart, the report shows the number of **site changes analyzed** and
the average **days between changes**. A site change, a pod change and a cannula
change all count.

### Glucose Pattern Around Site Changes

The vertical line in the chart is the moment of the site change. The chart
shows your glucose from 12 hours before the change to 24 hours after it.

- **The median line** is the middle glucose at each time.
- **The darker band** is the 25th to 75th percentile.
- **The lighter band** is the 10th to 25th and 75th to 90th percentile.
- **The target lines** mark the target range, 70 to 180 mg/dL.

The left side of the chart shows the hours before the change. A rise there can
mean that the old site absorbs insulin less well. The right side shows the
hours after the fresh site goes in.

Under the chart, four figures compare the two sides: **Avg before**, **Avg
after**, **TIR before** and **TIR after**. These are averages across all the
site changes in the period, not the result of one change.

The app shows glucose values in mmol/L if you chose mmol/L.

## How to use it

The report opens on the last 30 days. More site changes give a more reliable
pattern. To include more of them:

1. At the top of the report, select the date range.
2. Select **90 Days**.
3. Select **Apply Filters**. The report now uses all the site changes in 90
   days.
4. Look at the median line before and after the vertical line. Compare the
   shape with the shape for 30 days.

## Good to know

- The report needs at least 2 site changes in the period. With fewer, it shows
  **Insufficient Data** and the number of site changes it found.
- The report also needs enough glucose readings around the site changes. If
  there are too few, it shows **Insufficient Data**.
- The report finds site changes from the device events in your data. If your
  pump or app does not record site changes, log them in the
  [Treatment Log](/docs/reports/treatments).

<Callout type="info" title="A pattern to discuss">

  A steady rise in the hours before your site changes is a pattern to discuss
  with your care team. Do not change how often you change your site, or your
  doses, because of this report alone.

</Callout>
